<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Friedrich Ebert</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1871">1871</a>–<a href="/years/1925">1925</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Friedrich Ebert was a Social Democratic leader who became head of government when the German monarchy fell in November 1918 and, in February 1919, the first President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. He worked with the army to crush the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartacist-uprising">Spartacist uprising</a> and other left-wing risings, and defended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a> against the Kapp Putsch and the crisis of 1923. He died in office in 1925.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/friedrich-ebert.jpg" alt="Friedrich Ebert" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author or not provided · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_102-00015,_Friedrich_Ebert(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Friedrich Ebert was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> on 4 February 1871, the seventh of nine children of a tailor. His family could not afford university, so he trained as a saddle-maker from 1885 to 1888 and then travelled as a journeyman. He joined the Social Democratic Party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a> in 1889. He read <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a> but cared more for practical and organisational questions that would improve workers' lives.</p><p>After settling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> in 1891 he worked on a socialist newspaper, ran a pub that became a centre of socialist and union activity, and was elected to the Bremen assembly in 1900. In 1905 he became Secretary-General of the party and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, and in 1913, after August Bebel's death, he was elected party leader.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In August 1914 Ebert led the party's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> members to vote almost unanimously for war loans, regarding the war as a defensive measure against the autocratic regime of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a>. He backed the Burgfrieden, the truce among parties for the duration of the war, a stance that contributed to a split in 1917 when opponents of the war formed the Independent Social Democratic Party. Two of his four sons were killed in the war. In October 1918 he persuaded his party to join the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-maximilian-of-baden">Prince Maximilian of Baden</a>, arguing that it was a duty. He did not favour a republic, and told the prince that he hated social revolution like sin.</p><p>After the revolution reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> on 9 November 1918, Ebert and the party leadership sided with the civil service, army, police and judiciary against those who wanted a more radical revolution. He and General Wilhelm Groener planned the return of army units to Berlin in December. On 11 February 1919 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a>, meeting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>, elected him provisional president, and he was sworn in under the new constitution on 21 August 1919. He denounced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> as unbearable but accepted it after Hindenburg concluded the army could not resume the war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ebert suffered from gallstones. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> court in December 1924 fined a journalist who had called him a traitor, yet said that, in strict legal terms, he had committed treason by his role in the January 1918 strike. He gave evidence and delayed medical help. In February 1925 he underwent an emergency appendectomy and died of septic shock on 28 February, aged 54. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg">Paul von Hindenburg</a> succeeded him on 12 May. Historians remain divided. Some defend his actions as the price of preventing a socialist state on the model promoted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosa-luxemburg">Rosa Luxemburg</a> and Karl Liebknecht, while leftist critics call him a traitor who made the rise of the far right possible.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Friedrich Ebert” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Friedrich Ebert</b> charged Paul Hirsch of the MSPD with keeping order in Prussia, and on 12 November councils' representatives declared the old government deposed, promising to make Prussia a fully democratic part of the unified republic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-state-of-prussia">Free State of Prussia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 9 November 1918 Philipp Scheidemann of the Majority Social Democrats proclaimed the German Republic at the Reichstag building, the abdication of Kaiser Wilhelm II was announced, and Prince Max of Baden handed the chancellorship to <b>Friedrich Ebert</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 11 August 1922 President <b>Friedrich Ebert</b>, a Social Democrat, made the whole song the anthem of the Weimar Republic, as a political trade-off for the conservative right after the black, red and gold flag had been adopted in 1919, though he favoured singing the third stanza only.</blockquote><a href="/songs/deutschlandlied">Deutschlandlied</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Friedrich Ebert</b> signed the constitution of the new German republic, with universal suffrage and a strong president.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-11">11 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.41,8.7&amp;year=1906" aria-label="Heidelberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4750 442 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Friedrich Ebert"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5200 712h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5200 712h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.41,8.7&amp;year=1906">What was here in 1906? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
